Between subtle shading and the absence of light lies the nuance of iqlusion

(meaning)

This is an allusion to something. An allusion to what?

 

First, a technique used in painting, namely ‘sfumato’, which in Italian means ‘smoke’ (as in ‘smoke and mirrors’). In English it is translated to – ‘soft, vague or blurred’. It was used by the Italian high renaissance painters along with ‘cangiante, chiaroscuro and unione.’

 

Who used it most famously?

 

LEONARDO DA VINCI

 

And with what painting most famously?

 

THE MONA LISA

 

How so?

 

Well, what makes the Mona Lisa so famous is her smile, or lack thereof. Between the darkness in her eyes and the darkness on the sides of her lips, depending on how one views the painting she is either smiling or she is not. It is an illusion that has amazed, intrigued and puzzled people for over 500 years.

 

So, between subtle shading (the shading on the sides of her lips) and the absence of light (the dark on the sides of the eyes) is the nuance of iqlusion. But most importantly, this is an allusion to her nose. If you look at her nose you will most certainly see her not smiling (TRUTH or FACT). Anywhere else, and she is smiling. (Also look at the second line in the second CT portion of the copperplate to see NOHS NOS (NOSE) YAR are the eyes)

 

